From: François Guerraz <kubrick@fgv6.net>
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: tear down flows when a device loses
 carrier or changes upper

Flows are torn down when a device goes administratively down, but not
when it only loses carrier. A flow that transmits straight to such a
device keeps being refreshed by the packets it then drops: when the
cable of a bridge port is moved to another port of the same bridge,
downloads to the station behind it stall until the peer gives up, and a
UDP stream stalls for as long as the peer keeps sending.

A flow set up through a bridge port also outlives the port leaving the
bridge or joining another one, and keeps transmitting through the old
topology, marking its frames for TX forwarding offload into whatever
bridge the port is in now.

Also tear flows down on NETDEV_CHANGE when the device has no carrier,
and on NETDEV_CHANGEUPPER when the upper is a bridge or a LAG. The next
packet takes the slow path, and the flow is offloaded again along the
current path. Like NETDEV_DOWN, each such event walks every flowtable;
NETDEV_CHANGE does not say what changed, so this also runs for other
state changes while the device has no carrier.

The notifier lives in nf_flow_table_core.c here because of the pending
patch that moves it there; upstream it is in nft_flow_offload.c.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -826,8 +826,36 @@ static int nf_flow_table_netdev_event(struct notifier_block *this,
 {
 	struct net_device *dev = netdev_notifier_info_to_dev(ptr);
 
-	if (event != NETDEV_DOWN)
+	switch (event) {
+	case NETDEV_DOWN:
+		break;
+	case NETDEV_CHANGE:
+		/* A flow transmits to the device it was set up with for as
+		 * long as packets refresh it, and a device without carrier
+		 * drops them. Tear down the flows using the device, so that
+		 * the next packet takes the slow path and a new flow follows
+		 * the current topology, e.g. a station that moved to another
+		 * bridge port.
+		 */
+		if (netif_carrier_ok(dev))
+			return NOTIFY_DONE;
+		break;
+	case NETDEV_CHANGEUPPER: {
+		struct netdev_notifier_changeupper_info *info = ptr;
+
+		/* The device joined or left a bridge, or a LAG that can be in
+		 * one. A flow set up through the old topology could transmit
+		 * into the wrong bridge, or mark frames for a bridge the
+		 * device has left.
+		 */
+		if (!netif_is_bridge_master(info->upper_dev) &&
+		    !netif_is_lag_master(info->upper_dev))
+			return NOTIFY_DONE;
+		break;
+	}
+	default:
 		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+	}
 
 	nf_flow_table_cleanup(dev);
 
